Subject: [PATCH 29/30] ppc32 was the only arch built without -fno-builtin
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 13.3(C): "ppc's snprintf writes a byte and returns 0", for `%.0d` of
`0` only, with "MECHANISM NOT TRACED" written beside it.

    arch     writes   returns
    x86      "0"      1
    amd64    "0"      1
    ppc      "0"      0

It was never number(), and it was never ppc's Forth or its libc. Every
arch in this tree passes -fno-builtin -- amd64, x86, sparc32, sparc64,
ppc64, and even ppc-unix -- except one:

    ppc)  CFLAGS="-m32 -mcpu=604 -msoft-float \
                  -fno-builtin-bcopy -fno-builtin-log2 ..."

Two named exclusions where every sibling disables the lot. With snprintf
left as a GCC BUILTIN, a call with a constant format and a constant
argument lets the compiler compute the RETURN VALUE itself -- per C99,
where `%.0d` of `0` produces no characters -- while leaving the call in
place for its side effect. libc/vsprintf.c then performed that side
effect and wrote "0", because it diverges from C99 exactly there (TODO
13.3(B), `if (num == 0) tmp[i++] = '0';` runs unconditionally).

So the buffer came from our implementation and the return came from
GCC's. Two different printfs answering one call, and the arch that
disagreed was the one that let a second printf into the room.

MEASURED. Adding -fno-builtin to that one line, changing nothing else:

    before   d-zero ... wrote=1 ret=0 DIVERGES-AS-RECORDED RET-DISAGREES
    after    d-zero ... wrote=1 ret=1 DIVERGES-AS-RECORDED

which is what x86 and amd64 have always printed. The two named
exclusions are subsumed by -fno-builtin and are dropped.

A freestanding firmware that ships its own libc must not have the
compiler substituting its idea of that libc's semantics for the one on
disk -- and this is what that costs when it does: a divergence recorded
for a day as a defect in code that was innocent.

WATCHED TO BITE. The `diagnostics` track pinned the d-zero line PER ARCH
and went red on the fix, which is the good-news failure a
characterization test exists to produce. It now expects one line for all
three, and its RET-DISAGREES guard applies to every arch instead of
excusing ppc. Taking -fno-builtin away again fails it by name.

AND THE FIXTURE IS DELIBERATELY LEFT FOLDABLE. Passing the format through
a volatile pointer would stop a compiler answering for our libc -- and
would also hide the day a build flag lets one in again, which is exactly
what that guard is for.

diff --git a/config/scripts/switch-arch b/config/scripts/switch-arch
--- a/config/scripts/switch-arch
+++ b/config/scripts/switch-arch
@@ -267,7 +267,26 @@
         select_prefix powerpc powerpc64
         if [ "$unix" = "no" ]; then
             # 604 cpu includes support for PReP as well as Mac
-            CFLAGS="-m32 -mcpu=604 -msoft-float -fno-builtin-bcopy -fno-builtin-log2 -fno-stack-protector -fno-pic"
+            #
+            # LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). TODO 13.3(C): -fno-builtin, not the two
+            # named exclusions. This was the ONLY arch in the tree without full
+            # -fno-builtin -- amd64, x86, sparc32, sparc64, ppc64 and even
+            # ppc-unix all have it -- and it was the only arch where snprintf
+            # wrote a byte and returned 0.
+            #
+            # With snprintf left as a GCC builtin, a call with a constant format
+            # and constant argument lets the compiler compute the RETURN VALUE
+            # itself, per C99, while leaving the call in place for its side
+            # effect. libc/vsprintf.c's number() diverges from C99 for %.0d of 0
+            # (TODO 13.3(B)): it writes "0" where C99 writes nothing. So the
+            # buffer came from OUR implementation and the return came from
+            # GCC's -- "wrote 1 byte, returned 0" was two different printfs
+            # answering one call.
+            #
+            # A freestanding firmware that ships its own libc must not have the
+            # compiler substituting its idea of that libc's semantics for the
+            # one on disk. -fno-builtin subsumes the two exclusions below.
+            CFLAGS="-m32 -mcpu=604 -msoft-float -fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector -fno-pic"
             AS_FLAGS="-m32"
         else
             CFLAGS="-fno-builtin -fno-stack-protector"
